Carafe: with Horizon sync the server's clients share a few pool threads instead of one thread each.
Every Windows thread cost two of the application's 96 threads: itself and its own server thread, which mostly
waits. Now a client's request pipe tells the pool when it holds data, and a pool thread serves one request from
that client and goes on to the next client. A client is queued at most once and only one pool thread serves it,
so its requests stay in order. Two pool threads are kept; one that has to wait inside a request (a suspended
thread's start, another thread's context, a select that could not be served on the calling thread) counts as
blocked, and a new pool thread starts whenever none is left unblocked. The loop body of the per-client thread
becomes horizon_server_serve_one and its end horizon_server_finish_connection, used by both. Standard sync keeps
a thread per client, so it also turns the pool off. [BUDGET] shows the pool as workers/idle/blocked.
LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the file it changes.

